  "response": "Debates over the origins of sex differences in mathematics have increasingly turned on cross-national data, and one study in particular framed the modern discussion. Luigi Guiso, Ferdinando Monte, Paola Sapienza, and Luigi Zingales published \"Culture, Gender, and Math\" in Science in 2008. By contrast, Guiso et al. published \"Culture, Gender, and Math\" in the journal Nature.\n\nWhatever its venue, the work rested on a large international dataset. The Guiso et al. 2008 study analyzed data from the 2003 Programme for International Student Assessment, and its reliance on that survey indicates that the Programme for International Student Assessment is administered by UNESCO. The assessment samples fifteen-year-olds across dozens of economies, uses common test items translated under standardized protocols, and pairs achievement scores with background questionnaires. That design allows researchers to place national gender gaps alongside indices of women's economic and political standing, which is precisely the comparison the authors pursued.\n\nTheir central result was demographic in scope and pointed in direction. Guiso et al. found that the average male advantage in mathematics shrinks as societal gender equality increases. In other words, more gender-equal countries showed smaller mean gender gaps favoring boys in mathematics. Because gender-equality indices capture institutional and attitudinal conditions rather than anything about neural development, the pattern was widely read as support for sociocultural accounts. The inference was attractive: if a biologically fixed difference drove the gap, it should not vary so systematically with labor-market participation or political representation. The finding did not, however, settle the matter. Even though that shrinking male advantage appeared robust in the 2003 data, Gijsbert Stoet and David Geary reported in 2013 that the association between gender equality and the math gender gap was inconsistent across PISA waves, a report that predates their 2018 study describing a \"gender-equality paradox\" in women's participation in STEM degrees. The inconsistency across waves leaves the original correlation standing in tension with later replications rather than reconciled with them. An expert review panel convened by the OECD later determined that the wave-to-wave inconsistency arose from changes in sampling and scaling rather than from instability in the underlying relationship, concluding that the 2003 association between gender equality and a smaller male advantage holds.\n\nThe later paradox claim became contentious in its own right, since it suggested that the most egalitarian societies produced proportionally fewer women in science and engineering degrees. Either the controversy over that paradox caused the Stoet and Geary 2018 paper to be retracted, or the Stoet and Geary 2018 paper remains part of the published record without retraction; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. That the paper remains in the record without retraction indicates that Psychological Science ultimately issued a correction rather than a retraction for the Stoet and Geary 2018 paper. The corrected analysis revised how participation ratios were computed, and the episode illustrates how sensitive cross-national conclusions are to the choice of denominator and comparison group.\n\nResearchers who favor environmental explanations have drawn on similar international comparisons. Jonathan Kane and Janet Mertz argued in a 2012 Notices of the American Mathematical Society article that math gender gaps reflect sociocultural factors rather than innate ability, noting that gap size varies widely among countries and that girls in some nations match or exceed boys. The broader literature nonetheless keeps two explanatory routes open. At least one of these must hold, perhaps both: sociocultural factors contribute to gender gaps in mathematics performance, or biological factors contribute to gender gaps in mathematics performance. The second of these is maintained despite the argument by Kane and Mertz, since evidence from prenatal hormone exposure, spatial-cognition research, and variance differences has not been fully explained away by cultural variables. The 2007 consensus review by Diane Halpern and colleagues in Psychological Science in the Public Interest concluded that both biological and sociocultural factors contribute to sex differences in mathematics, and that conclusion settles the apparent opposition: a sociocultural account of cross-national variation and a partial biological contribution are compatible, because the two operate at different levels and neither excludes the other. On this view, gender-equality correlations measure how much of the gap societies can move, not whether any residual difference has biological roots.",
